{0 ReactiveData}

ReactiveData brings functional reactive programming to data structures that
change by small steps. Built on {{:https://erratique.ch/software/react}React},
it represents the changes of a list or a map as patches (insert this element,
remove that one) rather than as successive whole values, and the structures
computed from it are updated with the same patches: a change costs work in
proportion to its size, not to the size of the structure.

It is the basis of the reactive HTML of {{:https://ocsigen.org/tyxml/}TyXML}
and {{:https://ocsigen.org/eliom/}Eliom}, where an element whose children come
from a reactive list is updated in the page, child by child, when the list
changes.

{1 Overview}

- {!ReactiveData.RList}: reactive lists, the module you most likely need.
  {!ReactiveData.RList.create} returns a list and a handle to change it, with
  {!ReactiveData.RList.cons}, {!ReactiveData.RList.snoc},
  {!ReactiveData.RList.insert}, {!ReactiveData.RList.remove} or
  {!ReactiveData.RList.update}. {!ReactiveData.RList.map},
  {!ReactiveData.RList.filter}, {!ReactiveData.RList.concat} and
  {!ReactiveData.RList.rev} derive other reactive lists from it.
- {!ReactiveData.RMap}: reactive maps, over any [Map.S].
- {!ReactiveData.Make}: the reactive version of another data structure, given
  its patch format ({!ReactiveData.DATA}).

Reactive structures and React signals convert into each other:
{!ReactiveData.RList.from_signal} computes the patches between the successive
values of a signal, and {!ReactiveData.RList.signal} is the signal of the
current contents.

{1 Example}

{[
let l, h = ReactiveData.RList.create [ 1; 2; 3 ]
let squares = ReactiveData.RList.map (fun x -> x * x) l

(* Adding 4 at the end sends the patch to [squares], which computes the
   square of 4 only. *)
let () = ReactiveData.RList.snoc 4 h
let () = assert (ReactiveData.RList.value squares = [ 1; 4; 9; 16 ])
]}
